{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And HaShem said: My spirit shall not judge, etc.</b> This verse needs a herald [i.e., an explication], and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in it (Genesis Rabbah 26) many interpretations, but the plain sense of the verse is unknown. And it appears that it intends to say that since HaShem had conducted Himself with His creatures to reprove and to contend with them openly: ״וַיֹּאמֶר ה׳ אֶל הַנָּחָשׁ״ (Genesis 3:14), ״אֶל הָאִשָּׁה אָמַר״ etc. (Genesis 3:16), ״וּלְאָדָם אָמַר״ etc., ״וַיֹּאמֶר ה׳ אֶל קַיִן לָמָּה״ etc. (Genesis 4:10), ״וַיֹּאמֶר ה׳ אֵי הֶבֶל״ etc. (Genesis 4:9) — when they grew increasingly corrupt, HaShem said: My spirit shall not judge ever again the man, face to face, to be judged together with him." "And the meaning of <b>My spirit</b> is His Shekhinah, blessed be He — that the man would no longer be reckoned at this level. And you will find that according to his deeds He pushes his level lower and lower: at first HaShem would reprove the man to his face, and all the created beings were found at the level of prophets. <b>וַיְהִי כִּי הֵחֵל הָאָדָם</b> — meaning, he became profaned [נִתְחַלֵּל], or became ordinary [חֻלִּין], separated from the level of a prophet; and with the passage of time, ״the blossoms appeared in the earth״ — these are the righteous who restored the crown to its former state; and when the Temple was destroyed, vision was sealed and only the aspect of ruach ha-kodesh remained; and when the eyes of Israel were sealed shut, there is none among us who attains even the fragrance of holiness, and needless to say ruach ha-kodesh itself; and this is the tragedy of the house of Israel, than which there is nothing greater — to thirst to inhale the fragrance of our Father in heaven, and ״revive our spirit.״ And the beginning of this curse began from the generation of the Flood. And HaShem gave a reason for this: <b>בְּשַׁגַּם הוּא בָשָׂר</b> — meaning, because he added to corrupt himself with putrid corruption in the sin of adultery, he is בָּשָׂר [flesh], as stated in the matter; and HaShem hates immorality and abhors speaking to him." "<b>Further,</b> it intends along the path of Rabbi Isaac Arama's statement (of blessed memory), that the righteous transform matter into form, and the wicked transform form into matter, and this is their eternal destruction. And this is the meaning of <b>My spirit shall not judge</b> — meaning: He will have no desire [רָצוֹן] for them forever, meaning from now and forever, even in the eternal world; and the reason is <b>בְּשַׁגַּם</b> — meaning: because even the spirituality [רוּחָנִיּוּת] within him he reversed and made it <b>בָּשָׂר</b> [flesh], and this is the destruction of the soul. And this is what they said of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 108a): the generation of the Flood has no share in the World to Come." "<b>Further,</b> it intends along this path: <b>And HaShem said: it shall not judge ever again</b> — the attribute of mercy hinted at in the word <b>My spirit, forever</b>. The intention is that He will not relent from His decree upon them for evil, unlike His good practice whereby HaShem relents concerning evil, on the argument that they were fashioned from matter; as it says (Psalms 78:39) ״וַיִּזְכּוֹר כִּי בָשָׂר הֵמָּה״ [and He remembered that they were but flesh]. And this is the meaning of <b>בְּשַׁגַּם הוּא בָשָׂר</b> — meaning: even though he is בָּשָׂר, this argument and this defense will not avail them, to return to judge them with mercy." "<b>Further,</b> it intends: since HaShem fixed all reward and punishment for the supernal world, the wicked will return to Sheol. Now HaShem said that the judgment of the wicked will not be [reserved] for the World to Come — <b>בְּשַׁגַּם הוּא בָשָׂר</b> — meaning, because he is also בָּשָׂר, which is matter, and matter should be chastised in the turbid and coarse world — which is this lowly world. And He suspended for them one hundred and twenty years — perhaps they will return; and also, during this interval, the righteous who were in that generation, such as Methuselah and his peers, would complete their years. And you will find that they of blessed memory interpreted (Zohar vol. I 26a) the verse (Genesis 7:22) ״כֹּל אֲשֶׁר נִשְׁמַת רוּחַ וְגוֹ׳ מִכָּל אֲשֶׁר בֶּחָרָבָה מֵתוּ״ — that these are the righteous who possess the aspect of the soul [נְשָׁמָה] — all of them died before the coming of the Flood. End quote." "It further alludes that HaShem intended to say that He does not conduct absolute mercy in the world forever. This is His saying <b>וַיֹּאמֶר ה׳</b> — which is the name of mercy — <b>לֹא יָדוֹן רוּחִי</b>, meaning: the will of this designated name, which is appeased to show mercy, etc., shall not govern forever — meaning, for the created beings in the world. For in the very abundance of mercy, the created beings wax presumptuous to exalt themselves and pursue alien desires, along the lines of what He said (Hosea 2:10): ״וְכֶסֶף הִרְבֵּיתִי לָהּ״ and so forth. And HaShem removed the [attribute of] mercy to the supernal world. And you will find that even the righteous, even such as Jacob our father, did not attain the desire to settle in tranquility in this world, for the wrath of Joseph burst upon him (Genesis 37:2). And so every righteous person, when he sees that thirty days have passed over him in tranquility (Arakhin 16b), fears for himself, that he is not appeased to [i.e., by] the supernal world. And the reason is that HaShem so decreed: that the attribute of absolute mercy shall not be conducted in this world, even with the righteous. And this is His saying <b>בְּשַׁגַּם הוּא בָשָׂר</b>, meaning: from those who have a heart of flesh — in any case the attribute of judgment will pursue them to exact the strict accounting for transgressions. And through this, fear will multiply in the hearts of the created beings when He executes judgment upon the righteous and they behold it — and also the righteous, to bequeath them two worlds [שַׁ״י = שני עולמות]."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {5 "The abbreviation שַׁ״י is expanded as שני עולמות ('two worlds'), following the standard rabbinic idiom; the closing phrase לְהַנְחִילָם שַׁ״י עוֹלָמוֹת is read as 'to bequeath them two worlds.' The word מְרֻצֶּה ('appeased') appears twice with the same sense; in the first instance the phrase לְרַחֵם ('to show mercy') follows, and in the second instance לָעוֹלָם הָעֶלְיוֹן ('to the supernal world') follows, suggesting the meaning 'he is not reconciled with [the supernal world]'; translated hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:39:59.955008690Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}